  "exploiter_output": {
    "evidence": "Root cause / sink: org.ff4j.core.MappingUtil#instanceFlippingStrategy (ff4j-core/src/main/java/org/ff4j/utils/MappingUtil.java:181-190), specifically line 183 `Class.forName(className)`/`classLoader.loadClass(className)` immediately followed by line 184 `clazz.newInstance()` \u2014 the cast to `Class<FlippingStrategy>` at line 183 is an unchecked generic cast that provides NO runtime type safety; the JVM does not verify assignability until a FlippingStrategy method is actually invoked, by which point the attacker's constructor has already run. Trust boundary: `className` originates from untrusted config/API input (properties file values, XML attribute `class`, JSON `flip.class`, or the raw HTTP request parameter `strategy` in the web console) and reaches this sink without any allow-list or isAssignableFrom check. Verified live: unpatched run prints \"VULNERABLE\" and exits 0; after inserting `if (!FlippingStrategy.class.isAssignableFrom(rawClazz)) throw ...` before newInstance(), the same command prints \"NOT VULNERABLE\" and exits 1 (then reverted, confirmed identical to HEAD via git diff).\n\nEvery distinct sink/source-to-sink path in the alert that a complete fix must guard:\n1. ff4j-core/src/main/java/org/ff4j/utils/MappingUtil.java:181-190 `instanceFlippingStrategy` \u2014 Class.forName(className).newInstance() cast to FlippingStrategy. Reached from: PropertiesParser (properties `flip.strategy`/`flip.class` value, trace 2), FeatureJsonParser (JSON `flip.class`/classType field, traces in vuln #6, via MappingUtil.java:181-183 directly), and any other config-driven flip-strategy loader.\n2. ff4j-core/src/main/java/org/ff4j/conf/XmlParser.java:432-443 `parseFlipStrategy` \u2014 a SEPARATE, duplicated sink: `Class.forName(clazzName).newInstance()` cast to FlippingStrategy, driven by the XML `<flipstrategy class=\"...\">` attribute. Does not go through MappingUtil at all, so fixing MappingUtil alone will NOT close this path.\n3. ff4j-web/src/main/java/org/ff4j/web/embedded/ConsoleOperations.java:104-108 `createFeature` \u2014 a THIRD independent sink: `Class.forName(strategy).newInstance()` cast to FlippingStrategy, driven directly by the raw, unauthenticated-adjacent HTTP request parameter `strategy` from the embedded admin console (`req.getParameter(STRATEGY)`). This is the most directly attacker-reachable variant (network input, no parsing indirection).\n4. ff4j-core/src/main/java/org/ff4j/property/util/PropertyFactory.java:158-181 `createProperty(pName, pType, pValue, desc, fixedValues)` \u2014 sink is `Class.forName(pType).getConstructor(String.class, String.class).newInstance(pName, pValue)` at line 163-164, cast to `Property<?>`. `pType` reaches here from: PropertiesParser property `type=` values (via MappingUtil.mapPropertyType, trace 1), XmlParser property `type=` attribute (XmlParser.java:379-391, its own inline Class.forName+getConstructor call \u2014 a FOURTH, still separate duplication of the same pattern), PropertyJsonBean/MongoPropertyMapper (trace 5, path 1), FeatureJsonParser embedded custom-property JSON (trace 5, paths 2-3), and PropertyJsonParser (trace 5, path 4). All funnel into PropertyFactory.createProperty as the common downstream sink, but XmlParser.java:387 is an independent duplicate that must also be fixed even if PropertyFactory is hardened.\n5. ff4j-core/src/main/java/org/ff4j/utils/MappingUtil.java:93-98 `mapPropertyType` is NOT itself a sink (pure string substitution / passthrough for unrecognized values) but is the shared \"trust laundering\" hop that lets a raw attacker string flow unchanged into sinks #4 whenever it doesn't match a known short alias \u2014 a fix here (e.g. rejecting unknown types outright) is one valid mitigation layer but does not substitute for validating at the actual construction sinks.\n6. ff4j-core/src/main/java/org/ff4j/utils/MappingUtil.java:180-183 `className` param used by trace in vuln #6 (FeatureJsonParser flip `classType`) reaches the same MappingUtil sink as #1 \u2014 confirms MappingUtil.instanceFlippingStrategy is the converging sink for both properties- and JSON-sourced flip-strategy class names, but XmlParser (#2) and ConsoleOperations (#3) remain independent code paths.",
    "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "MappingUtil.instanceFlippingStrategy(\"pov-uid\", \"org.ff4j.pov.MappingUtilInjectionPoV$NotAFlippingStrategy\", initParams) reflectively resolves and instantiates the attacker-named class via Class.forName(className).newInstance() BEFORE ever checking it is assignable to FlippingStrategy. The class's constructor runs (flips a static AtomicBoolean) even though it does not implement FlippingStrategy at all; only afterward does the implicit cast fail and get swallowed into a FeatureAccessException. The PoV observes the side-effect flag and prints \"VULNERABLE\" + exits 0. After a fix adds an isAssignableFrom (or allow-list) check prior to newInstance(), the attacker class is never constructed, the flag stays false, the PoV prints \"NOT VULNERABLE\" and exits 1.",
    "notes": "Targeted alert trace: vulnerability #2 (PropertiesParser flip-strategy trace, PropertiesParser.java:307\u2192311 `flipStrategyClass`) and, by extension, its convergence point ff4j-core/utils/MappingUtil.java:181-190 `instanceFlippingStrategy`, which is also the terminal sink named directly in vulnerability #6's JSON traces (MappingUtil.java:181-183 `className`). I chose this sink over the PropertyFactory/PropertyType traces (vulnerabilities #1, #3, #5) because it is the simplest, most self-contained demonstration of the alert's stated root cause (\"Expected base-type constraints must hold at every construction sink\") \u2014 it requires only a no-arg constructor (no JDK gadget class or two-String-arg constructor hunting needed), it is 100% network/dependency-free to exercise (no XML/JSON parsing scaffolding, no servlet container), and it directly calls real, unmodified production code (org.ff4j.utils.MappingUtil, compiled from ff4j-core) rather than simulating the behavior. I validated the PoV both ways: it exits 0 (vulnerable) against the current HEAD, and exits 1 (blocked) after temporarily adding an `isAssignableFrom` guard at the sink, then reverted that temporary edit (confirmed via `git diff` against HEAD showing zero difference) so the worktree's tracked files are unmodified \u2014 only the new `.security-pipeline/pov/` directory is added. The `evidence` and `regression_hint` fields above enumerate the four other structurally-independent sinks (XmlParser's two separate Class.forName call sites, ConsoleOperations' HTTP-driven site, and PropertyFactory's (String,String)-constructor site) that a downstream patcher must also fix, since this PoV exercises only one of them.",
    "pov_command": "bash .security-pipeline/pov/run_pov.sh",
    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/src/org/ff4j/pov/MappingUtilInjectionPoV.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce, at EVERY one of the sinks listed above, before any constructor of the resolved class executes: the resolved `Class<?>` must be validated (via `isAssignableFrom` against the expected base type \u2014 `FlippingStrategy` for strategy sinks, `Property` for property-type sinks \u2014 or via an explicit allow-list of known implementation class names) BEFORE calling `newInstance()` / `Constructor.newInstance()`. Doing the check only via a post-construction cast (as today) is insufficient because the attacker's no-arg or (String,String) constructor has already run by the time the ClassCastException surfaces \u2014 any side effects (file writes, network calls, JNDI lookups, static initializers) already fired.\n\nKnown bypass variants the fix must also block, since each is a structurally-independent call site and patching only one leaves the others exploitable:\n- MappingUtil.instanceFlippingStrategy (ff4j-core/utils/MappingUtil.java:183-184) \u2014 properties- and JSON-sourced flip-strategy class names.\n- XmlParser.parseFlipStrategy (ff4j-core/conf/XmlParser.java:443) \u2014 XML `<flipstrategy class=\"...\">` attribute, duplicate Class.forName+newInstance logic, not routed through MappingUtil.\n- ConsoleOperations.createFeature (ff4j-web/web/embedded/ConsoleOperations.java:107-108) \u2014 raw HTTP request parameter `strategy`/`strategyParams`, the most directly network-reachable variant.\n- PropertyFactory.createProperty (ff4j-core/property/util/PropertyFactory.java:163-164) \u2014 reflective (String,String)-constructor instantiation keyed by `pType`, reachable from properties, XML, JSON (FeatureJsonParser, PropertyJsonParser), and Mongo-mapped PropertyJsonBean inputs.\n- XmlParser property `type=` attribute handling (ff4j-core/conf/XmlParser.java:379-391) \u2014 a second, independent inline duplicate of the PropertyFactory reflective-construction pattern; fixing PropertyFactory alone does not cover this because XmlParser calls `Class.forName(optionalType).getConstructor(...).newInstance(...)` itself rather than delegating to PropertyFactory.\n\nAlso verify the fix doesn't rely solely on string-based checks that are easy to bypass (e.g. checking `className.startsWith(\"org.ff4j.\")` can be defeated by any third-party or classpath-local class under that prefix, or by classes registered via a custom ClassLoader set through `MappingUtil.setClassLoader`); prefer `isAssignableFrom` reflective checks plus, ideally, an explicit allow-list of concrete strategy/property classes shipped or explicitly registered by the application, applied uniformly across all five sinks above.",
